WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:04.310
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:10.610
Whenever we build a website, a video game,
or do any sort of photography and image manipulation,

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:18.160
we often encounter the problems of image downscaling,
decolorization, and HDR tone mapping.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:23.050
This work offers us one technique that can
do all three of these really well.

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:28.039
But first, before we proceed, why are we talking
about downscaling?

00:00:28.039 --> 00:00:33.210
We are in the age of AI, where a computer
program can beat the best players in Chess

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:37.800
and Go, so why talk about such a trivial challenge?

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.710
Well, have a look here.

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:44.871
Imagine that we have this high-fidelity input
image and due to file size constraints, we

00:00:44.871 --> 00:00:47.469
have to produce a smaller version of it.

00:00:47.469 --> 00:00:50.840
If we do it naively, this is what it looks
like.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:52.469
Not great, right?

00:00:52.469 --> 00:00:58.239
To do a better job at this, our goal would
be that the size of the image would be reduced,

00:00:58.239 --> 00:01:02.640
but while still retaining the intricate details
of this image.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:09.330
Here are two classical downsampling techniques…better,
but the texture of the skin is almost completely

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:10.330
lost.

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:11.930
Have a look at this!

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:15.010
This is what this learning-based technique
came up with.

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:16.200
Really good, right?

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.400
It can also perform decolorization.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:25.330
Again, a problem that sounds trivial for the
unassuming Scholar, but when taking a closer

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:30.370
look, we notice that there are many different
ways of doing this, and somehow we seek a

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:36.630
decolorized image that still relates to the
original as faithfully as possible.

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:40.880
Here you see the previous methods that are
not bad at all, but this new technique is

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:45.210
great at retaining the contrast between the
flower and its green leaves.

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:50.710
At this point it is clear that deciding which
output is the best is highly subjective.

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:52.770
We’ll get back to that in a moment.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:57.190
It is also capable of doing HDR tone mapping.

00:01:57.190 --> 00:02:01.960
This is something that we do when we capture
an image with a device that supports a wide

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:07.620
dynamic range, in other words, a wide range
of colors, and we wish to display it on our

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:11.319
monitor, which has a more limited dynamic
range.

00:02:11.319 --> 00:02:14.430
And again, clearly there are many ways to
do that.

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:17.680
Welcome to the wondrous world of tone mapping!

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:21.790
Note that there are hundreds upon hundreds
of algorithms to perform these operations

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:23.859
in computer graphics research.

00:02:23.859 --> 00:02:29.180
And also note that these are very complex
algorithms that took decades for smart researchers

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:30.640
to come up with.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:35.749
So the seasoned Fellow Scholar shall immediately
ask - why talk about this work at all?

00:02:35.749 --> 00:02:38.349
What’s so interesting about it?

00:02:38.349 --> 00:02:43.569
The goal here is to create a little more general,
learning-based method that can do a great

00:02:43.569 --> 00:02:47.530
job at not one, but all three of these problems.

00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:49.439
But how great exactly?

00:02:49.439 --> 00:02:52.630
And how do we decide how good these images
are?

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:57.180
To answer both of these questions at the same
time, if you’ve been watching this series

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:03.209
for a while, then you are indeed right, the
authors created a user study, which shows

00:03:03.209 --> 00:03:09.420
that for all three of these tasks, according
to the users, the new method smokes the competition.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:14.190
It is not only more general, but also better
than most of the published techniques.

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:18.889
For instance, Reinhard’s amazing tone mapper
has been an industry standard for decades

00:03:18.889 --> 00:03:25.540
now, and look, almost 75% of the people prefer
this new method over that.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:32.040
What required super smart researchers before
can now be done with a learning algorithm.

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:33.250
Unreal.

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:34.250
What a time to be alive.

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:39.620
A key idea for this algorithm is that this
convolutional neural network you see on the

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:45.430
left is able to produce all three of these
operations at the same time, and to perform

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:51.560
that, it is instructed by another neural network
to do this in a way that preserves the visual

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:53.909
integrity of the input images.

00:03:53.909 --> 00:03:58.310
Make sure to have a look at the paper for
more details on how this perceptual loss function

00:03:58.310 --> 00:03:59.310
is defined.

